**Feature flags** (also called feature toggles or feature switches) represent a fundamentally different approach to managing deployment risk. The other three strategies control *where* traffic goes — which version of the binary receives the request. Feature flags control *what code executes inside that binary*. The new code is shipped to production in an inactive state; the old code path continues to run. Activating the new feature is a runtime configuration change, not a deployment.

**The core pattern** is deceptively simple: a conditional check at the boundary of new functionality, evaluated against a flag service at request time:

```javascript
if (featureFlags.isEnabled('new-checkout-flow', { userId: user.id })) {
  return newCheckoutController.handle(req);
}
return legacyCheckoutController.handle(req);
```

The `featureFlags.isEnabled()` call queries a flag service (LaunchDarkly, Unleash, Flagsmith, or a simple database row) and returns a boolean. The flag service can evaluate rules: return `true` for all users in the `beta-testers` group, return `true` for 10% of all users (using a hash of the user ID for consistency — the same user always gets the same experience), return `true` only in the `staging` environment, and so on.

**Dark launches** are a particularly powerful pattern: deploy the new code path to production with the flag off, then enable it for internal employees only. They exercise the code against real production infrastructure — real databases, real network topology, real traffic patterns — without any external user exposure. This lets you catch infrastructure-level issues (connection pool exhaustion, cache miss patterns, slow database queries) before you release to users.

**A/B testing** is feature flags applied to product decisions rather than engineering decisions. Route 50% of users to variant A and 50% to variant B, measure conversion rates, and use the winner. The feature flag infrastructure is identical; what changes is the motivation and the metrics you are evaluating.

**Kill switches**: The most important feature of a flag is the ability to turn it off instantly. A kill switch does not require a deployment, a pull request, or even a terminal — it is a UI toggle in the flag service dashboard. When a severe incident is traced to a specific feature, flipping the kill switch rolls back the user experience in seconds, regardless of how many servers are running and whether a Kubernetes rollout is possible.

**The flag lifecycle problem**: Feature flags accumulate as technical debt if not managed. A flag added for a risky launch that is now universally enabled becomes a dead code branch — the false path will never execute again, but it stays in the codebase, confusing future readers and interacting unexpectedly with new flags. Best practice is to treat flag removal as a first-class engineering task: create a ticket for flag cleanup at the time you create the flag, with a target date after the feature is fully rolled out.

